[{:db/id 17592186416862 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{1,3,5,7\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,3),(1,5),(3,5),(5,7)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nWe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,1){{field-1}}R,\\,(3,3){{field-2}}R,\\,(5,5){{field-3}}R,\\,(7,7){{field-4}}R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{1,3,5,7\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R,\\,(5,5)\\notin R,\\,(7,7)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(x,x)\\notin R$ $\\text{for all }x\\in A,$ the relation is $\\text{irreflexive}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416853 :question/problem "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}.$ Given that $R$ is reflexive, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix.\n\n|  | $\\alpha $ | $\\beta $ | $\\gamma $ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\alpha $ | {{field-1}} | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $\\beta $ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |\n| $\\gamma $ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416859 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{5,8,10\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(5,5),(5,8),(8,10),(10,5),(10,8)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{5,8,10\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(5,5)\\in R,\\,(8,8)\\notin R,\\,(10,10)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(8,8)\\notin R$ $\\text{and }(5,5)\\in R,$ the relation is $\\text{neither reflexive nor irreflexive}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416854 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{0,1,2\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(0,0),(0,1),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{0,1,2\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(0,0)\\in R,\\,(1,1)\\in R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(2,2)\\notin R$ $\\text{and }(1,1)\\in R,$ the relation is $\\text{neither reflexive nor irreflexive}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416852 :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{△,♢,♡\\}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\{(△,♡),(△,♢),(♢,♡)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nWe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(△,△){{field-1}}R,\\,(♢,♢){{field-2}}R,\\,(♡,♡){{field-3}}R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n{{field-4}}{{field-5}},\n\nthe relation is\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{△,♢,♡\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(△,△)\\notin R,\\,(♢,♢)\\notin R,\\,(♡,♡)\\notin R.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(x,x)\\notin R$ $\\text{for all }x\\in A,$ the relation is $\\text{irreflexive}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416855 :question/problem "Which of the following digraphs represent a reflexive relation defined on the set $A=\\{-1,0,1\\}?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416856 :question/problem "Which of the following digraphs represent a reflexive relation defined on the set $A=\\{\\pi ,\\omega ,ϕ\\}?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416863 :question/problem "![](images/03/03ddf06402ad38708d232cf28783c817bf881965d54650eb158e7ffca4ea76a2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204235 :question/problem "Which of the following digraphs represent a relation, defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\},$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186420406 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cddd6584fee52757b1c7a470a6eeffee3678d569e36025ca70c99e5543c71c2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent a relation, defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta \\},$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
